## Task
Write a structured characterization of this run's system-wide CPU utilization. Organize your response
into the following sections:

### 1. Phase Overview
Identify the distinct time-phases visible in the chart (a phase is a stretch of time where `cpu` is
either clearly high/busy or clearly low/idle, bounded by a visible transition in the image -- a repeated
duty-cycle pattern of several busy bursts separated by idle gaps counts as several phases, not one).
For each phase, give its approximate time range, whether it's busy or idle, and one sentence of
interpretation. Present this as a Markdown table: `| Phase | Time range (s) | Busy or idle | Notes |`.

### 2. Transitions
Describe each transition between phases: roughly when it happens (from the image), how sharp it is
(an abrupt step vs. a gradual ramp), and whether `iowait`/`irq` show any activity around it worth
calling out (a workload that's blocked on I/O rather than computing looks different from one that's
simply between bursts).

### 3. Utilization Summary
Using the numeric summary table (not the image), state the mean `cpu` value over the whole run and
whether that's consistent with the image's overall visual impression (e.g. a low mean with a duty-cycle
shape means "busy in short bursts", not "lightly loaded throughout" -- these read very differently in a
chart even when the mean is the same). Note whether `iowait` or `irq` have any non-trivial mean/max,
which would suggest I/O-bound or interrupt-heavy behavior rather than pure CPU-bound compute.
